Z||000006||loafer87gt||08-23-2000||02:44 AM||||Kinda reminds me of this spring when my car was idling bad and it was in the shop for over a month. GM charged me over $1600 for parts that didn't fix the problem, and thanks to Ogre and the forum, me and my basically non-existent mechanical skills tracked the problem down to a bad tach filter. I was very pissed off at them to say the least, but they refused to refund any of my money for the parts that they replaced while trying to guess the problem. I'll never even think about buying another GM in my lifetime after dealing with their dealerships. Their mechanics and service reps are about as useless as tits on a boar.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||08-23-2000||02:56 AM||||A few years ago when ford redesigned the V-6 in the taurus, My uncle bought one. It had a funny noise coming from the trans on the drive home from the dealer. So the next day he took it back to them and it was a bad switch in the trans. They had to pull the trans out to replace it. Not a big deal BUT the morons dropped the engine/trans assembly!<BR>I guess the hoist broke. Anyhow it somehow damaged the engine and they were going to replace it. Since it was a new designed engine it took 4 months for the dealership to aquire one! They refused to buy the car back. They insisted on fixing it. At least they gave him a loaner car for the 4 months.<BR>It's not just GM it's all car manufacturers that treat people badly.||||reg||1
Z||000008||frontal lobe||08-24-2000||12:57 AM||||I sent an e-mail to Chevrolet and got an e-mail back from Jessica Floyd!!  I didn't even mention the guys name, just the situation.  She sent back an e-mail mentioning his name and website!  You could tell it wasn't a form letter.  Too bad they didn't pay that much attention from the beginning.<P>I love gm engines and transmissions.  Too bad their ecm's and electronics are garbage (my experience, and his too).  Sad to say, but I guess I'll stick with my Mazdas and Nissans.  At least my Nissan was built in the U.S., unlike the "American-made" Impala built in Canada.  (Hey, I'm not making a back-handed flame at our Canadian friends, just saying that all American cars aren't built in the U.S. and some "foreign" cars are).||||reg||1
